  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n\nCurrently quasi-zonal flow is over the Northern Plains, with a\nbroad ridge over the Southern Plains. The severe storms in the\nJames River Valley this morning have mostly moved into South\nDakota. At the surface weak high pressure is in the region. \n\nThe severe storm risk today looks very conditional. There is\nplenty of CAPE with dew points in the 60s and 70s across the\nsouth, however the Bismarck 18z sounding shows a strong cap. So\nthe majority of the CAPE (2727 J/kg) is elevated. What might\nstart the event before sunset is the convective temperature is \n84, however there is plenty wildfire smoke aloft that might \nprohibit that. Shear across the area is around 50kts so \norganized. Most of the CAMS keep convection happening until \nclose to sunset when maybe the jet stream speed max connects \nwith them. There is some storms starting to fire on the Black \nHills, but that is near the stationary front. Whatever boundary \nkicked off storms in our east is still there and has some \ncumulus trying but currently hitting the cap. If or when storms \ngo the hazards are 2 inch hail and 70mph winds. Thunderstorms \nare possible through tonight and into the morning, especially in\nthe south central. \n\nThe rest of this week will be much cooler, below average in\nfact, in the 70s. The upper pattern will turn northwest\nWednesday, before turning to the southwest Thursday. Daily\nchances of showers and thunderstorms are possible, including\novernight through Saturday morning in the active southwest flow.\nThere is currently a level 1 risk of severe storms again\nWednesday in the south, with current hazards being golf balls\nand 70mph winds. It is possible that over an inch of rain could\nfall between today and Saturday morning across our forecast\narea. Thursday and Friday look very breezy from a pressure\ngradient, near Advisory level. This weekend looks warmer in \nthe lower 80s, then maybe back to average early next week.",
